  module: "Module 4: Connecting Accelerators into a Cluster",
  title: "Switches and Network Interfaces — How Data Finds a Path Across the Cluster",
  date: "2026-09-20",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 040: GPUs handle many workloads, while custom chips can be more efficient when their specialized design matches the job.

Lesson 041: Compilers and kernels turn model operations into hardware work, so better software keeps more of the accelerator productive.

Lesson 042: A faster accelerator costs less only when its extra useful output outweighs its higher hourly price in the real workload.

Lesson 043: Scale-up tightly connects a limited group of accelerators, while scale-out links many servers and racks into a larger cluster.

Lesson 044: Collective operations let many accelerators combine or share data, and their communication pattern determines how long the group waits.

The big idea

A network interface is the server's doorway into the cluster network. A switch is the traffic junction that moves each packet toward the correct doorway.

Together they turn a collective operation from software intent into electrical or optical signals crossing real hardware. The slowest usable segment—not the fastest component on the spec sheet—sets the transfer rate.

Where we are

GPU memory → PCIe path → network interface → cable or optical link → switch fabric → remote network interface → remote GPU memory

1. Follow one piece of an all-reduce

Suppose a GPU has calculated part of a training gradient. The collective library divides that data into chunks and tells the communication system where each chunk must go.

Inside a server, the chunk first travels from GPU memory toward a network interface card, or NIC. In InfiniBand, the corresponding device is often called a host channel adapter, or HCA. Both are endpoint devices: they connect the server to the network.

The NIC turns a memory transfer into network packets, adds destination and ordering information, transmits the bits through a port, receives returning packets and places their data in the right memory location.

A switch sits between endpoints. It reads enough packet information to select an outgoing port, then moves the packet through its internal switching fabric. Another link, switch and NIC may follow before the chunk reaches its destination.

The return path can repeat this process many times during one collective.

2. The NIC does more than provide a socket

A high-speed NIC contains its own packet-processing logic, queues, memory-access engines and ports. It also connects to the server through a host interface such as PCI Express.

Remote direct memory access, or RDMA, lets a NIC move data to or from registered memory without asking the CPU to copy every chunk. NVIDIA's GPUDirect RDMA provides a direct PCIe path between GPU memory and a peer device such as a network interface.

“Direct” does not mean path-free. The transfer can still be limited by the GPU's ability to expose data, PCIe topology, NIC processing, port speed or software setup. NVIDIA's documentation notes that a path containing only PCIe switches is preferable to one crossing CPU interconnects.

This matters because avoiding an unnecessary bounce through host memory can save bandwidth and latency, but it cannot make a 400-gigabit port carry 800 gigabits per second.

3. The switch forwards many flows at once

A switch has many ports connected by a switching chip. For each packet, it chooses an output, buffers the packet if that output is temporarily busy and schedules it for transmission.

If two packets want the same output at the same moment, both cannot leave at full speed through one port. One waits in a buffer. Persistent excess traffic fills queues, raises latency and can eventually force backpressure or packet recovery.

